-- title:
--   Commutativity of the Hecke operators on J_H(M)
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $M \ne 0$ and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$, and let $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ be `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Write $F =$ [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79) for the intermediate field `xHFunctionFieldC ℚ M H` of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$, and $\bar F =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ $F$, the subfield of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise image of $F$; then [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127) is $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\bar F$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. the group of degree-zero divisors modulo the subgroup of principal divisors. For a prime $\ell$, the additive endomorphism [`ModularCurve.heckeOperatorHAlong`](def/ModularCurve_XHHeckeOperator.html#L195) $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ $M$ $H$ $\ell$ of this group is, by definition, the correspondence $\alpha_* \circ \beta^*$ attached to `heckeBetaHBar` and `heckeAlphaHBar` via `Pic0.correspondence` whenever the conjunction of inputs `HeckeInputsHAlong` holds (the $\beta$-map being defined, integrality of $\alpha$ and $\beta$, existence of principal divisors on the base-changed top field for level $M\ell$, finiteness along $\alpha$, the fundamental identity along $\beta$ and the norm formula along $\alpha$), and is the zero endomorphism otherwise. The assertion is that for all primes $\ell, \ell'$ and every $P \in$ [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), applying the operator for $\ell$ after that for $\ell'$ at $P$ gives the same class as applying the operator for $\ell'$ after that for $\ell$.
--
--   This is the classical commutativity of the Hecke correspondences $T_\ell$ (written $U_\ell$ when $\ell \mid M$) on the Jacobian of the modular curve $X_H(M)$, here transported to degree-zero divisor classes over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$. It underlies the commutativity of the algebra generated by Hecke and diamond operators acting on the Tate module of $J_H(M)$, and is used for the commuting-operator statements and the trace and determinant computations on that Tate module.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_heckeOperatorHAlong_comm.lean

